<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/expert/comparative-legal-systems">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Comparative law studies <strong>how different legal systems solve the same human
    problems</strong> — contracts, property, crime, family. Two great families dominate (common
    law and civil law), with religious and customary systems woven through much of the world, and
    mixed jurisdictions proving the borders are porous. Comparison is how lawyers see their own
    system's choices as choices.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the families, the blends, and the convergence" widget="equationStep" config={compCfg} />

  <h2>What actually differs in practice</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Style of judgment:</strong> common-law judgments argue through precedent and reasoning at length; civil-law judgments are shorter, citing codes. Same case, different literary form.</li>
    <li><strong>Judicial role:</strong> common-law judges shape law; civil-law judges are (formally) appliers — though constitutional courts everywhere have made judges central anyway.</li>
    <li><strong>Juries, procedure and costs:</strong> adversarial trials with juries (common law) vs inquisitorial investigating judges (civil tradition) — the deepest practical difference citizens notice.</li>
    <li><strong>Transplants work slowly:</strong> borrowing a rule without its institutions fails (the classic critique — Watson vs Legrand's "legal culture" debate). Law travels with its society, or not at all.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Zweigert & Kötz, An Introduction to Comparative Law; Watson, Legal Transplants; Glenn, Legal Traditions of the World.</p>
</TopicPage>
